<BaseLayout title="Statistical Mechanics" description="Ensembles, partition functions, Fermi occupations, entropy, finite-temperature free energies, and fluctuations for electronic structure." current="theory">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/theory/')}>Theory</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Statistical Mechanics</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Statistical Mechanics</h1>
  <p class="lede">Statistical mechanics connects microscopic states to equilibrium occupations, entropy, free energies, fluctuations, and collective thermodynamic behavior.</p>
  <p>For electronic structure it is essential when interpreting metals, finite electronic temperature, phonon free energies, phase stability, magnetic disorder, and thermal response. It must remain distinct from numerical smearing, molecular dynamics sampling, and macroscopic thermodynamics.</p>

  <h2>An ensemble states what is controlled and what fluctuates</h2>
  <p>The canonical partition function at temperature <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Z equals trace exponential minus beta H">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Tr</mi><mspace width="0.2em"/><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\mathrm&#123;Tr&#125;\,e^&#123;-\beta\hat&#123;H&#125;&#125;,\quad\beta=1/(k_&#123;\mathrm&#123;B&#125;&#125;T).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The microcanonical, canonical, and grand-canonical ensembles fix different combinations of energy, particle number, volume, temperature, and chemical potential. Their equivalence requires a thermodynamic limit and appropriate regularity; finite systems and phase coexistence can retain ensemble-specific behavior.</p>
  <p>When particle number can exchange with a reservoir, the grand partition function and grand potential are</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Xi equals trace exponential minus beta H minus mu N and Omega equals minus k B T log Xi">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>Ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Tr</mi><mspace width="0.2em"/><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>(</mo><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mover><mi>N</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo>
          <mspace width="0.8em"/><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>Ξ</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi=\mathrm&#123;Tr&#125;\,e^&#123;-\beta(\hat&#123;H&#125;-\mu\hat&#123;N&#125;)&#125;,\qquad\Omega=-k_&#123;\mathrm&#123;B&#125;&#125;T\ln\Xi.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The reservoir, conserved quantities, and allowed particle-number sectors must be declared. A computational occupation parameter does not by itself define a physical grand-canonical experiment.</p>

  <h2>Free energies are generated from partition functions</h2>
  <p>The Helmholtz free energy follows from</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="F equals minus k B T log Z">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=-k_&#123;\mathrm&#123;B&#125;&#125;T\ln Z.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Changing ensemble changes the thermodynamic potential. Internal energy, Helmholtz free energy, Gibbs free energy, and grand potential answer different constrained minimization questions and cannot be substituted by one electronic total energy.</p>

  <h2>Fermi–Dirac occupations connect bands to finite-temperature electron counts</h2>
  <p>For independent fermionic levels, the mean occupation is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="f epsilon equals one over exponential beta epsilon minus mu plus one">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\varepsilon)=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;e^&#123;\beta(\varepsilon-\mu)&#125;+1&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The chemical potential is fixed by the particle-number constraint and generally varies with temperature. Metals require dense sampling near the Fermi surface because small occupation changes can affect energies, forces, response, and entropy.</p>

  <h2>Bose–Einstein occupations describe nonconserved bosonic modes</h2>
  <p>For a bosonic mode of energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="hbar omega"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbar\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and zero chemical potential, the equilibrium occupation is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="n B omega equals one over exponential beta hbar omega minus one">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mi>ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_&#123;\mathrm&#123;B&#125;&#125;(\omega)=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;e^&#123;\beta\hbar\omega&#125;-1&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Phonons in thermal equilibrium usually use zero chemical potential because their number is not conserved. Other bosonic systems can require a chemical potential when an associated number is conserved or externally constrained. Bose occupation does not impose Pauli exclusion, so low-energy modes can acquire large populations.</p>

  <h2>Electronic entropy is part of a finite-temperature variational problem</h2>
  <p>For independent fermionic occupations,</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="S equals minus k B sum f log f plus one minus f log one minus f">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mo></mo></munderover><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>ln</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=-k_&#123;\mathrm&#123;B&#125;&#125;\sum_i[f_i\ln f_i+(1-f_i)\ln(1-f_i)].</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Mermin's finite-temperature extension of density-functional theory makes the equilibrium density minimize a grand-potential functional at stated temperature and chemical potential. This formal temperature should not be silently identified with an arbitrary convergence aid.</p>

  <h2>Numerical smearing and physical temperature must remain separate</h2>
  <p>Smearing can regularize Brillouin-zone integration and SCF convergence in metals. Some schemes approximate a finite electronic temperature; others are quadrature devices with correction formulas and no direct thermal interpretation.</p>
  <p>Report the occupation scheme, width or temperature, extrapolated or free-energy quantity, k-point mesh, and target observable. Convergence with respect to one smearing width does not establish physical finite-temperature behavior.</p>

  <h2>Lattice vibrations add a distinct thermal contribution</h2>
  <p>In the harmonic approximation, a phonon mode of frequency <math class="math-inline" aria-label="omega q nu"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> contributes a free energy</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="F ph equals sum half hbar omega plus k B T log one minus exponential minus beta hbar omega">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>ph</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo></mo></munderover><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_&#123;\mathrm&#123;ph&#125;&#125;=\sum_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;[\tfrac12\hbar\omega_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;+k_&#123;\mathrm&#123;B&#125;&#125;T\ln(1-e^&#123;-\beta\hbar\omega_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;&#125;)].</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Imaginary harmonic modes, anharmonicity, thermal expansion, disorder, and phase transitions can invalidate a simple harmonic free-energy comparison. Electronic and vibrational thermal terms are separate approximations with separate convergence requirements.</p>

  <h2>Fluctuations connect response to equilibrium correlations</h2>
  <p>Susceptibilities, heat capacities, compressibilities, and transport coefficients can be expressed through fluctuations or correlation functions under stated ensembles. This connection motivates linear response and many-body Green-function methods, but a finite trajectory or finite cell samples only an approximation to the ensemble.</p>

  <h2>Phase transitions require thermodynamic-limit and finite-size reasoning</h2>
  <p>Nonanalytic free energies and true spontaneous symmetry breaking are thermodynamic-limit statements. Finite cells and finite trajectories usually round singularities, restrict correlation lengths, and can switch among competing states only on sampled timescales.</p>
  <p>A phase-transition claim therefore needs an order parameter or response criterion, finite-size or correlation-length analysis, equilibration and sampling checks, and control of the relevant ensemble. One heat-capacity peak or one abrupt change in a single simulation cell is not by itself a thermodynamic phase boundary.</p>

