  <h2>Study routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/">MIT OpenCourseWare 8.333, <cite>Statistical Mechanics I</cite></a> is a graduate route through thermodynamics, probability, ensembles, interacting systems, quantum gases, phonons, and Fermi/Bose statistics. Its prerequisites and breadth exceed the minimum needed for routine ground-state DFT.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Zt4y1E7GV/"><cite lang="zh-CN">热力学统计物理：华中科技大学邹远川 2022</cite></a> is a clearly marked original Chinese course sequence covering thermodynamic laws, canonical and grand-canonical reasoning, transport preliminaries, ideal Fermi and Bose gases, phase transitions and critical phenomena, and introductory irreversible thermodynamics. It is a coherent undergraduate route for learners who need Chinese explanations before the more compact graduate texts. It does not turn a numerical smearing width into a physical temperature or prescribe finite-temperature DFT, molecular-dynamics sampling, or phase-boundary calculations.</p>
  <p><a href="https://journals.aps.org/pr/abstract/10.1103/PhysRev.137.A1441">N. David Mermin, “Thermal Properties of the Inhomogeneous Electron Gas”</a> is the primary formal source for finite-temperature density-functional theory in the grand canonical ensemble.</p>
  <p>Konstantin Likharev’s openly licensed <a href="https://open.umn.edu/opentextbooks/textbooks/part-sm-statistical-mechanics"><cite>Statistical Mechanics</cite></a> is an independent, text-first graduate bridge from thermodynamics to physical statistics, gases, phase transitions, and fluctuations. Readers who already handle derivatives and elementary quantum mechanics can use it to distinguish an ensemble from a single microscopic configuration and to connect fluctuation language with electronic and ionic degrees of freedom. Its CC BY-NC-SA terms and Stony Brook publication are explicit; it is not a finite-temperature DFT implementation guide and its kinetics chapter should not be read as a general nonequilibrium workflow.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>This page does not prescribe a universal smearing, electronic temperature, phonon free-energy model, molecular-dynamics ensemble, or phase-stability workflow. It also does not treat one converged finite-temperature calculation as proof that all relevant thermal degrees of freedom have been included.</p>
</BaseLayout>
